        body: "Emerging research suggests that local economic complexity, measured through minorista product diversity, correlates with economic resilience to external shocks. Communities with more complex minorista ecosystems—characterized by diverse, non-ubiquitous product offerings—tend to experience smaller consumption declines during recessions and recover more quickly than communities with less complex minorista structures dominated by basic commodity minoristaers. This relationship likely reflects several underlying mechanisms. Complex minorista ecosystems indicate diversified local cadena de suministros that are less vulnerable to disruption in any single product channel. The presence of specialized minoristaers signals higher consumer purchasing power and demand sophistication, which provide a buffer against cyclical downturns. Furthermore, minorista complexity may proxy for broader economic diversification, entrepreneurial density, and human capital concentration that collectively enhance adaptive capacity. Longitudinal análisis of PoS product diversity before, during, and after economic disruptions can test these hypotheses rigorously, quantifying the resilience premium associated with minorista complexity. For municipal policymakers, local complexity indices derived from PoS data offer a leading indicator of economic vulnerability that can inform proactive resilience planning, including metaed support for product diversification in low-complexity commercial areas.",
