        body: "Integrating PoS demand signals into trade facilitation processes faces several implementation challenges. Data aggregation across minoristaers of different sizes, sectors, and technology platforms requires standardization of product classification systems to enable meaningful comparison with trade nomenclatures such as the Harmonized System codes used in customs declarations. The mapping between minorista product categories and trade commodity classifications is non-trivial: a single imported commodity may appear under dozens of minorista product names and categories, while a minorista product category may encompass goods from multiple trade classification headings. Coverage representativeness is another concern—PoS-equipped minoristaers may not represent the full domestic demand picture, particularly in economies with large informal minorista sectors. Statistical methods must account for the share of total domestic demand captured by the PoS data source to aanulación misleading demand estimates. Data governance frameworks must address the sensitivity of sharing aggregated minorista demand intelligence with customs and trade authorities. While anonymized and aggregated at the sector level, PoS demand data could potentially reveal competitive intelligence about specific importers or minorista chains if insufficiently anonymized. Clear protocols governing the aggregation level, access controls, and permitted uses of PoS-derived trade intelligence are essential to secure comerciante participation and maintain data quality. International coordination to develop standards for PoS-trade data integration would accelerate adoption and enable cross-country punto de referenciaing of trade-minorista alignment.",
